 `torch.cat` allocation+copy (the advertised \"memory-bandwidth bottleneck\") and makes the split free — we write each GEMM straight into its own output. `W` stays hot across both streams.\n\n**The decisive constraint — numerics:** outputs are length-3072 dot products (magnitude ≈55), and the budget is `atol≈0.002 + rtol·55 ≈ 0.00255`.\n- Plain **TF32** → error ≈0.04, ~16× over budget → **rejected**.\n- **tf32x3** → near-fp32 (~5e-5 error), tensor-core speed (~52 TFLOPS eff) → **primary**.\n- **ieee** fp32 → exact, ~19.5 TFLOPS → **accuracy fallback**.\n\nThe tight atol also implies the reference is true fp32, which is what makes a ~2.7× tensor-core speedup ceiling plausible; c001 will empirically confirm this.\n\n**Workloads:** `M_total` ranges 768→5824; encoder streams can be tiny (`M=77`) while image streams reach 4096 — so thin-M efficiency and L2 residency of `W` (37.7 MB ≈ A800's 40 MB L2) are the tuning concerns.\n\n**Design space, validation, candidate roadmap, and skill applicability** (KernelWiki/ncu are Hopper/Blackwell-only → not applicable to sm_80) are all documented.\n\nPer the workflow and your instruction, I stopped after the draft — no `docs/plan.md` and no solution code were created.","ttft_ms":9433,"ttft_stream_ms":9433,"time_to_request_ms":61,"type":"result","duration_ms":503727,"uuid":"49908c3d-a367-450b-955c-01fee525ffd0"}
