{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LR8DJowVASWPmEZYZHqMdg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t    \"name\": \"030_flux_concatenated_sequence_processing_with_split\",\n3\t    \"hf_id\": \"black-forest-labs/FLUX.1-Kontext-dev\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Flux concatenated sequence processing pattern: concatenates text and image sequences, applies linear projection, then splits back. This pattern appears in dual-stream transformer blocks and represents a memory bandwidth bottleneck.\",\n5\t    \"axes\": {\n6\t        \"batch_size\": {\n7\t            \"type\": \"var\",\n8\t            \"description\": \"Batch size\"\n9\t        },\n10\t        \"text_seq_len\": {\n11\t            \"type\": \"var\",\n12\t            \"description\": \"Text/encoder sequence length\"\n13\t        },\n14\t        \"img_seq_len\": {\n15\t            \"type\": \"var\",\n16\t            \"description\": \"Image sequence length\"\n17\t        },\n18\t        \"hidden_dim\": {\n19\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n20\t            \"value\": 3072,\n21\t            \"description\": \"Hidden dimension size\"\n22\t        }\n23\t    },\n24\t    \"custom_inputs_entrypoint\": null,\n25\t    \"inputs\": {\n26\t        \"hidden_states\": {\n27\t            \"shape\": [\n28\t                \"batch_size\",\n29\t                \"img_seq_len\",\n30\t                \"hidden_dim\"\n31\t            ],\n32\t            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n33\t            \"description\": \"Image latent sequence\"\n34\t        },\n35\t        \"encoder_hidden_states\": {\n36\t            \"shape\": [\n37\t                \"batch_size\",\n38\t                \"text_seq_len\",\n39\t                \"hidden_dim\"\n40\t            ],\n41\t            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n42\t            \"description\": \"Text conditioning sequence\"\n43\t        },\n44\t        \"process_weight\": {\n45\t            \"shape\": [\n46\t                \"hidden_dim\",\n47\t                \"hidden_dim\"\n48\t            ],\n49\t            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n50\t            \"description\": \"Linear projection weight matrix\"\n51\t        }\n52\t    },\n53\t    \"outputs\": {\n54\t        \"processed_encoder\": {\n55\t            \"shape\": [\n56\t                \"batch_size\",\n57\t                \"text_seq_len\",\n58\t                \"hidden_dim\"\n59\t            ],\n60\t            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n61\t            \"description\": \"Processed encoder hidden states\"\n62\t        },\n63\t        \"processed_hidden\": {\n64\t            \"shape\": [\n65\t                \"batch_size\",\n66\t                \"img_seq_len\",\n67\t                \"hidden_dim\"\n68\t            ],\n69\t            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n70\t            \"description\": \"Processed image hidden states\"\n71\t        }\n72\t    },\n73\t    \"reference\": \"import torch\\n\\n@torch.no_grad()\\ndef run(\\n    hidden_states: torch.Tensor,\\n    encoder_hidden_states: torch.Tensor,\\n    process_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n) -> tuple[torch.Tensor, torch.Tensor]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Flux concatenated sequence processing pattern.\\n    \\n    1. Concatenate encoder_hidden_states and hidden_states along sequence dimension\\n    2. Apply linear projection to the concatenated sequence\\n    3. Split back into separate encoder and image streams\\n    \\n    Args:\\n        hidden_states: Image latent sequence [batch, img_seq_len, hidden_dim]\\n        encoder_hidden_states: Text conditioning sequence [batch, text_seq_len, hidden_dim]\\n        process_weight: Linear projection weight [hidden_dim, hidden_dim]\\n        \\n    Returns:\\n        Tuple of (processed_encoder_hidden_states, processed_hidden_states)\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    text_seq_len = encoder_hidden_states.shape[1]\\n    img_seq_len = hidden_states.shape[1]\\n    \\n    # Step 1: Concatenate sequences along sequence dimension\\n    # Shape: [batch, text_seq_len + img_seq_len, hidden_dim]\\n    concatenated = torch.cat([encoder_hidden_states, hidden_states], dim=1)\\n    \\n    # Step 2: Apply linear projection (no bias)\\n    # processed = concatenated @ process_weight.T\\n    processed = torch.matmul(concatenated, process_weight.t())\\n    \\n    # Step 3: Split back into separate streams\\n    processed_encoder = processed[:, :text_seq_len, :]\\n    processed_hidden = processed[:, text_seq_len:, :]\\n    \\n    return processed_encoder, processed_hidden\\n\"\n74\t}\n75\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bcbb7765-926a-4d22-97a5-e083cb94ef12","uuid":"c3f63807-c15f-4de5-8a8d-2fd75a14c283","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:59:11.267Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-030_flux_concatenated_sequence_processing_with_split/task/definition.json","content":"{\n    \"name\": \"030_flux_concatenated_sequence_processing_with_split\",\n    \"hf_id\": \"black-forest-labs/FLUX.1-Kontext-dev\",\n    \"description\": \"Flux concatenated sequence processing pattern: concatenates text and image sequences, applies linear projection, then splits back. This pattern appears in dual-stream transformer blocks and represents a memory bandwidth bottleneck.\",\n    \"axes\": {\n        \"batch_size\": {\n            \"type\": \"var\",\n            \"description\": \"Batch size\"\n        },\n        \"text_seq_len\": {\n            \"type\": \"var\",\n            \"description\": \"Text/encoder sequence length\"\n        },\n        \"img_seq_len\": {\n            \"type\": \"var\",\n            \"description\": \"Image sequence length\"\n        },\n        \"hidden_dim\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 3072,\n            \"description\": \"Hidden dimension size\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"custom_inputs_entrypoint\": null,\n    \"inputs\": {\n        \"hidden_states\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_size\",\n                \"img_seq_len\",\n                \"hidden_dim\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n            \"description\": \"Image latent sequence\"\n        },\n        \"encoder_hidden_states\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_size\",\n                \"text_seq_len\",\n                \"hidden_dim\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n            \"description\": \"Text conditioning sequence\"\n        },\n        \"process_weight\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"hidden_dim\",\n                \"hidden_dim\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n            \"description\": \"Linear projection weight matrix\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"outputs\": {\n        \"processed_encoder\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_size\",\n                \"text_seq_len\",\n                \"hidden_dim\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n            \"description\": \"Processed encoder hidden states\"\n        },\n        \"processed_hidden\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_size\",\n                \"img_seq_len\",\n                \"hidden_dim\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n            \"description\": \"Processed image hidden states\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"reference\": \"import torch\\n\\n@torch.no_grad()\\ndef run(\\n    hidden_states: torch.Tensor,\\n    encoder_hidden_states: torch.Tensor,\\n    process_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n) -> tuple[torch.Tensor, torch.Tensor]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Flux concatenated sequence processing pattern.\\n    \\n    1. Concatenate encoder_hidden_states and hidden_states along sequence dimension\\n    2. Apply linear projection to the concatenated sequence\\n    3. Split back into separate encoder and image streams\\n    \\n    Args:\\n        hidden_states: Image latent sequence [batch, img_seq_len, hidden_dim]\\n        encoder_hidden_states: Text conditioning sequence [batch, text_seq_len, hidden_dim]\\n        process_weight: Linear projection weight [hidden_dim, hidden_dim]\\n        \\n    Returns:\\n        Tuple of (processed_encoder_hidden_states, processed_hidden_states)\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    text_seq_len = encoder_hidden_states.shape[1]\\n    img_seq_len = hidden_states.shape[1]\\n    \\n    # Step 1: Concatenate sequences along sequence dimension\\n    # Shape: [batch, text_seq_len + img_seq_len, hidden_dim]\\n    concatenated = torch.cat([encoder_hidden_states, hidden_states], dim=1)\\n    \\n    # Step 2: Apply linear projection (no bias)\\n    # processed = concatenated @ process_weight.T\\n    processed = torch.matmul(concatenated, process_weight.t())\\n    \\n    # Step 3: Split back into separate streams\\n    processed_encoder = processed[:, :text_seq_len, :]\\n    processed_hidden = processed[:, text_seq_len:, :]\\n    \\n    return processed_encoder, processed_hidden\\n\"\n}\n","numLines":75,"startLine":1,"totalLines":75}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fv5ddU9Nh813yuLwDmZf3K","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft — L2/030 Flux Concatenated Sequence Processing with Split\n2\t\n3\tRun: `formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-030_flux_concatenated_sequence_processing_with_split`\n4\tTarget GPU: NVIDIA A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere). Framework: Triton primary; PyTorch only for\n5\tmetadata/launch plumbing. No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension computational fallback.\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## 1. Operation summary\n10\t\n11\tReference (`task/definition.json`) does, with `H = hidden_dim = 3072` (const):\n12\t\n13\t```\n14\tconcatenated = torch.cat([encoder_hidden_states, hidden_states], dim=1)  # [B, T+I, H]\n15\tprocessed    = concatenated @ process_weight.t()                          # [B, T+I, H]\n16\tprocessed_encoder = processed[:, :T, :]                                   # [B, T, H]\n17\tprocessed_hidden  = processed[:, T:, :]                                   # [B, I, H]\n18\treturn processed_encoder, processed_hidden\n19\t```\n20\t\n21\tSymbols:\n22\t- `B` = `batch_size` (var), `T` = `text_seq_len` (var), `I` = `img_seq_len` (var).\n23\t- Inputs: `hidden_states` `[B, I, H]` fp32, `encoder_hidden_states` `[B, T, H]` fp32,\n24\t  `process_weight` `W` `[H, H]` fp32 (an `nn.Linear`-style weight, `out=H`, `in=H`, no bias).\n25\t- Outputs: `processed_encoder` `[B, T, H]` fp32, `processed_hidden` `[B, I, H]` fp32.\n26\t\n27\tThe task card calls this a **memory-bandwidth bottleneck** pattern from Flux dual-stream\n28\ttransformer blocks: the `cat` materializes a fresh `[B, T+I, H]` tensor before the GEMM and the\n29\t`split` slices it afterward.\n30\t\n31\t---\n32\t\n33\t## 2. Core algebraic simplification (the main lever)\n34\t\n35\tA linear projection is applied **independently per token row**. Concatenating along the sequence\n36\t(row) dimension, projecting, then splitting back along the same row boundaries is a pure\n37\tre-ordering that does **not** couple encoder rows with image rows. Therefore:\n38\t\n39\t```\n40\tprocessed_encoder = encoder_hidden_states @ W.T      # [B, T, H]\n41\tprocessed_hidden  = hidden_states         @ W.T      # [B, I, H]\n42\t```\n43\t\n44\tis **mathematically identical** (row for row, elementwise) to the concat→matmul→split reference,\n45\twith the exact same output ordering. Consequences:\n46\t\n47\t- The `torch.cat` allocation + copy is **eliminated entirely** (this is the advertised bottleneck).\n48\t- The `split` becomes trivial — we write each GEMM result straight into its own output tensor;\n49\t  no post-hoc slicing/copy.\n50\t- Both streams share the **same weight `W`**, so `W` stays hot in cache/registers across both.\n51\t\n52\tBoth projections are the same GEMM shape family:\n53\t```\n54\tX [M, K] · W^T [K, N]  ->  Y [M, N],   with  K = N = H = 3072\n55\t```\n56\twhere `X` is a stream flattened to 2-D (`[B*T, H]` for encoder, `[B*I, H]` for image), and\n57\t`W` is read as `W[n, k]` (i.e. `Y = X @ W.T`, per `nn.Linear`). Rows are fully independent, so\n58\tbatch and sequence collapse into a single `M` dimension for contiguous inputs.\n59\t\n60\t**Equivalence conditions to preserve:**\n61\t- Row order within each stream is preserved by flattening `[B, S, H] -> [B*S, H]` in C-contiguous\n62\t  order and reshaping the output back the same way.\n63\t- Encoder output uses encoder rows; image output uses image rows — never crossed.\n64\t- Must produce **two** separate output tensors of the exact reference shapes/dtype.\n65\t\n66\t---\n67\t\n68\t## 3. Workload analysis (fixed feedback set)\n69\t\n70\t`H = 3072`, so every GEMM has `K = N = 3072`. `M_enc = B*T`, `M_img = B*I`,\n71\t`M_total = B*(T+I)` (the reference's concatenated row count).\n72\t\n73\t| WL | uuid(prefix) | B | T | I | M_enc | M_img | M_total | atol | rtol | match |\n74\t|----|--------------|---|-----|-----|-------|-------|---------|-------|-------|-------|\n75\t| 1  | 7ae93ff7 | 2 | 1423 | 1489 | 2846 | 2978 | 5824 | 0.0021 | 1e-5 | 0.98 |\n76\t| 2  | 7a91d659 | 4 | 128  | 256  | 512  | 1024 | 1536 | 0.002  | 1e-5 | 0.98 |\n77\t| 3  | a9d58452 | 1 | 77   | 1024 | 77   | 1024 | 1101 | 0.0018 | 1e-5 | 0.98 |\n78\t| 4  | 9de8abcb | 2 | 128  | 256  | 256  | 512  | 768  | 0.002  | 1e-5 | 0.98 |\n79\t| 5  | e92f1fac | 1 | 77   | 4096 | 77   | 4096 | 4173 | 0.002  | 1e-5 | 0.98 |\n80\t\n81\tObservations:\n82\t- `M_total` spans **768 → 5824** rows; `N = K = 3072` throughout. These are medium GEMMs, all\n83\t  compute-heavy (large K,N) but with modest-to-small M.\n84\t- Encoder streams can be **very small** (`M_enc = 77` in WL3/WL5) — a thin GEMM. The image stream\n85\t  dominates FLOPs there. A single tiling config must remain efficient for both a 77-row and a\n86\t  4096-row GEMM.\n87\t- FLOPs per workload `= 2 * M_total * N * K`:\n88\t  - WL1: `2·5824·3072·3072 ≈ 1.10e11` (110 GFLOP)\n89\t  - WL2: `≈ 2.90e10`, WL3: `≈ 2.08e10`, WL4: `≈ 1.45e10`, WL5: `≈ 7.87e10`.\n90\t- Tensor sizes (fp32, MB): `X_total = M_total·3072·4`; `W = 3072·3072·4 = 37.7 MB`.\n91\t  - WL1: `X ≈ 71.5 MB` per side (in+out ≈ 143 MB) plus `W` 37.7 MB.\n92\t  - `W` (37.7 MB) is close to A800 L2 (40 MB) — reuse of `W` across all `M` tiles is essential;\n93\t    large `BLOCK_M` and good L2 residency of `W` matter.\n94\t\n95\t**Roofline sanity (A800):** peak fp32 SIMT ≈ 19.5 TFLOPS; TF32 tensor ≈ 156 TFLOPS; HBM ≈ 2 TB/s.\n96\t- True-fp32 SIMT GEMM for WL1 ≈ `1.10e11 / 19.5e12 ≈ 5.6 ms`.\n97\t- TF32-tensor GEMM for WL1 ≈ `0.7 ms` (but inaccurate — see §5).\n98\t- 3×TF32 emulation (`tf32x3`) ≈ 1/3 of TF32 throughput ≈ 52 TFLOPS eff. → WL1 ≈ `2.1 ms`.\n99\t- `cat` traffic for WL1 ≈ read(enc+img) + write(concat) ≈ `2 · 71.5 MB ≈ 143 MB` → ≈ `0.07 ms`\n100\t  of pure bandwidth that our fused path deletes; relatively larger for the small workloads.\n101\t\n102\t---\n103\t\n104\t## 4. Constraints\n105\t\n106\t- **Dtype:** all fp32 in and out. Output tensors must be fp32 with reference shapes.\n107\t- **Triton-only compute:** the GEMM must run in a Triton kernel. PyTorch may allocate outputs,\n108\t  reshape/view, and launch. No `torch.matmul`/`F.linear`/cuBLAS as the computational path, no\n109\t  CPU/NumPy, no CUDA C extension, no \"if Triton fails use torch\" fallback. A failed Triton\n110\t  implementation is invalid, not fallback-able.\n111\t- **Arch:** `sm_80`. No `tcgen05`/TMEM/wgmma/Hopper-Blackwell features. Ampere `mma` (incl.\n112\t  TF32 tensor path) is the ceiling. `tl.dot` with fp32 inputs is the vehicle.\n113\t- **Evaluation:** only `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`. Five workloads = one\n114\t  candidate evaluation. Budget 100 evals; token soft 1.0M / hard 1.2M. Ranking = geomean speedup,\n115\t  every selected workload must pass correctness. No direct CUDA/profiler/nvidia-smi runs.\n116\t\n117\t---\n118\t\n119\t## 5. Numerical risks (decisive for design)\n120\t\n121\tRandom `~N(0,1)` inputs; each output element is a length-3072 dot product, so\n122\t`Var ≈ 3072`, i.e. output magnitude `≈ 55`. The pass rule (standard) is\n123\t`|out - ref| ≤ atol + rtol·|ref|` for ≥ 98% of elements. Effective budget for `|ref| ≈ 55`:\n124\t`≈ 0.002 + 1e-5·55 ≈ 0.00255`.\n125\t\n126\tPrecision options for `tl.dot` on fp32 inputs (Ampere):\n127\t\n128\t1. **Plain TF32 (`input_precision=\"tf32\"`)** — operands truncated to 10-bit mantissa\n129\t   (`~5e-4` relative). Error of a 3072-term dot ≈ `sqrt(3072)·(~7e-4)·O(1) ≈ 0.04`, i.e.\n130\t   **~0.04 absolute — ~16× over the 0.00255 budget on essentially every element.**\n131\t   → **Rejected.** Fast (~0.7 ms WL1) but fails correctness badly.\n132\t\n133\t2. **3×TF32 (`input_precision=\"tf32x3\"`)** — decomposes each fp32 operand into 3 TF32 terms;\n134\t   accuracy ≈ near-fp32 (relative `~1e-6`, i.e. absolute `~5e-5` at magnitude 55). **Comfortably\n135\t   inside 0.00255.** Throughput ≈ 1/3 of TF32 tensor ≈ 52 TFLOPS eff → clearly beats fp32 SIMT.\n136\t   → **Primary choice.** Best accuracy/speed trade-off.\n137\t\n138\t3. **IEEE fp32 (`input_precision=\"ieee\"`)** — true fp32 via CUDA-core FMA (no fp32 tensor core on\n139\t   Ampere), `~19.5` TFLOPS. Exactly matches the reference numerically; same speed class as cuBLAS\n140\t   fp32. Only wins from removing the `cat`. → **Fallback precision** if `tf32x3` is unavailable in\n141\t   the installed Triton, or as an accuracy-safety candidate.\n142\t\n143\t**Why the reference must be true fp32 (not TF32):** with `atol ≈ 0.002` on magnitude-55 outputs,\n144\tno tensor-core approximation could reproduce a TF32 reference to that tolerance. The tight atol\n145\tis only self-consistent if the reference is computed in true fp32 and the atol is the *budget*\n146\tgranted to approximate implementations. This is exactly the margin `tf32x3` needs and plain TF32\n147\tblows. It also implies our `tf32x3` result (≈ true fp32) will land near the reference — safe.\n148\t\n149\t**Dependency / risk:** if, contrary to the above, the harness reference secretly ran with\n150\t`allow_tf32=True` (TF32), then a near-fp32 `tf32x3` result could differ from it by `~0.04` and\n151\tfail the 98% match. The tolerance analysis argues strongly against this, but the **first\n152\tevaluation of c001 is the check**: if `tf32x3` fails correctness, that scenario (or a strides/\n153\tshape bug) is the cause and we revisit. Accumulation is fp32 in all three modes (Triton accumulates\n154\t`tl.dot` in fp32), so accumulation order is not a concern.\n155\t\n156\tOther numerical notes:\n157\t- No bias, no activation, no reductions across tokens → no additional cancellation beyond the dot.\n158\t- 2% mismatch slack (`0.98`) gives margin, but `tf32x3` should pass ~100% of elements.\n159\t- Keep everything fp32; do not down-cast the accumulator or outputs.\n160\t\n161\t---\n162\t\n163\t## 6. Triton design space\n164\t\n165\t### 6.1 Kernel structure — tiled GEMM (`Y = X @ W.T`)\n166\tStandard 2-D tiled matmul:\n167\t- Program grid over `(M, N)` tiles: `BLOCK_M × BLOCK_N` output tile, reduce over `K` in\n168\t  `BLOCK_K` chunks; accumulate in fp32; `tl.dot(x_tile, w_tile, acc, input_precision=...)`.\n169\t- `W` read as `W[n, k]` so that `Y = X·W^T`; equivalently load `W` transposed tiles. Choose the\n170\t  load pattern (via strides) that keeps `W` accesses coalesced; `W` is `[H, H]` contiguous.\n171\t- `L2`-friendly tile scheduling (grouped-M / \"super-grouping\" of program ids) to maximize `W` and\n172\t  `X` reuse — important because `W` (37.7 MB) barely fits L2.\n173\t- Boundary masking on `M` (streams like `M=77` are not multiples of `BLOCK_M`); `N=K=3072` is a\n174\t  multiple of common block sizes (128/64), so N/K masking may be avoidable for speed.\n175\t\n176\t### 6.2 Handling the two streams (launch strategy)\n177\t- **Option A (baseline, simplest): two kernel launches**, one per stream, each over its flattened\n178\t  `[M_side, H]`, writing directly into `processed_encoder` / `processed_hidden`. `W` is reused hot\n179\t  across both launches. Launch overhead is negligible vs GEMM. **Preferred starting point.**\n180\t- **Option B: one launch over a \"virtual\" concat.** A single grid of `M_total` row-tiles, where a\n181\t  tile reads from encoder or image tensor depending on the global row index, and writes to the\n182\t  matching output. Saves one launch but needs per-tile source selection (two base pointers +\n183\t  branch or index math). Marginal benefit; consider only if launch overhead shows up for the\n184\t  small workloads.\n185\t- **Option C: batched/grouped GEMM.** Treat `(B, stream)` as groups. Overkill here since flatten\n186\t  already collapses `B` into `M` for contiguous inputs; keep in reserve for non-contiguous cases.\n187\t\n188\tFor all options, avoid materializing the concatenation and avoid slicing copies.\n189\t\n190\t### 6.3 Precision knob\n191\t- Start with `input_precision=\"tf32x3\"` (accuracy-safe, tensor-core speed).\n192\t- Keep `input_precision=\"ieee\"` as an alternative candidate for an accuracy floor / if `tf32x3`\n193\t  is missing in the installed Triton.\n194\t- Never use plain `\"tf32\"` for the committed solution (fails tolerance) — could be used only as a\n195\t  throwaway diagnostic to confirm the accuracy/speed trade-off, but not submitted.\n196\t\n197\t### 6.4 Tiling / occupancy search axes (for later candidates, not this draft)\n198\t- `BLOCK_M ∈ {64, 128, 256}`, `BLOCK_N ∈ {64, 128, 256}`, `BLOCK_K ∈ {32, 64, 128}`.\n199\t- `num_warps ∈ {4, 8}`, `num_stages ∈ {2, 3, 4, 5}` (software pipelining of K-loop; watch shared\n200\t  memory pressure with fp32 tiles on `sm_80`, which has 164 KB/SM configurable smem).\n201\t- Grouped-M scheduling `GROUP_M ∈ {4, 8}` for L2 reuse.\n202\t- Consider a **thin-M specialization** for tiny encoder streams (`M=77`): small `BLOCK_M` (e.g. 64)\n203\t  to avoid wasting rows, or fold the small stream differently. Possibly different configs per\n204\t  stream (encoder vs image) since their `M` differ by 1–2 orders of magnitude.\n205\t- `tf32x3` roughly triples the effective K-work; `BLOCK_K` and pipelining interact with that.\n206\t\n207\t### 6.5 What NOT to do\n208\t- No split-K unless a workload proves K-parallelism starved (K=3072 with these M/N is fine and\n209\t  split-K needs an fp32 atomic/second-pass reduction that risks accuracy/complexity).\n210\t- No fp16/bf16 down-cast (blows tolerance).\n211\t- No cuBLAS/`torch.matmul` compute path.\n212\t\n213\t---\n214\t\n215\t## 7. Baseline cost model & expected speedup\n216\t\n217\tReference cost ≈ `cat` (bandwidth) + `matmul` (true-fp32 SIMT, ~19.5 TFLOPS) + `split` (views,\n218\t≈ free). Our fused path ≈ `matmul` (`tf32x3`, ~52 TFLOPS eff) with **no** `cat`, writing outputs\n219\tdirectly.\n220\t\n221\t- Matmul speedup ceiling ≈ `52 / 19.5 ≈ 2.7×` (if reference is true-fp32 SIMT and cuBLAS is not\n222\t  itself tensor-accelerated). Plus the deleted `cat` traffic (a few percent on large WL, more on\n223\t  the small WL where the GEMM is short and copies are relatively bigger).\n224\t- Realistic geomean target: meaningful (> ~1.5×) if the reference is true fp32; the exact number\n225\t  depends on how close cuBLAS fp32 SIMT is to peak and on our tiling efficiency at small M.\n226\t- **Falsifiable checkpoint:** if c001 (`tf32x3`, fused) is *slower* than the reference, the most\n227\t  likely explanation is that the reference matmul is already tensor-accelerated (TF32) — in which\n228\t  case our accuracy would also be at risk and we would re-examine assumptions rather than chase\n229\t  tiling.\n230\t\n231\t---\n232\t\n233\t## 8. Validation strategy\n234\t\n235\tConstraints: no direct CUDA/profiler/nvidia-smi/alternate harness; the trusted evaluator is the\n236\tonly correctness+timing oracle, invoked as `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` over\n237\tall five fixed workloads (= one evaluation).\n238\t\n239\tLayered validation:\n240\t1. **Algebraic equivalence (static, this draft):** concat→matmul→split ≡ per-stream matmul; row\n241\t   order preserved by C-contiguous flatten/reshape; two outputs of exact reference shape/dtype.\n242\t   Documented above (§2).\n243\t2. **Shape/stride audit before each eval:** confirm handling of `[B, S, H]` contiguous inputs,\n244\t   flatten to `[B*S, H]`, reshape outputs back to `[B, S, H]`; handle `W` as `[H, H]` with correct\n245\t   transpose semantics; guard `T=0`/`I=0` (not in feedback set but cheap to guard). Prefer passing\n246\t   strides to the kernel over forcing `.contiguous()` copies; only copy if a stream is\n247\t   non-contiguous (feedback inputs are expected contiguous).\n248\t3. **Numerical margin reasoning (§5):** `tf32x3` error `~5e-5` ≪ budget `~0.00255`; expect ~100%\n249\t   element match. c001 doubles as the empirical confirmation that the reference is true fp32.\n250\t4. **Evaluator-driven loop:** each immutable candidate `cNNN` evaluated once over the 5 workloads;\n251\t   record per-workload correctness (must pass) and speedup, geomean, decision, cumulative eval\n252\t   count, parent, source hash, hypothesis, skill usage in `candidates.jsonl` (append-only).\n253\t5. **Regression discipline:** change one axis per candidate (precision → then tiling → then launch\n254\t   strategy) so each eval attributes cause cleanly; never reuse an id for changed source.\n255\t6. **Accuracy safety net:** keep an `ieee`-precision variant ready; if any workload fails\n256\t   correctness under `tf32x3`, evaluate the `ieee` variant to isolate accuracy vs. bug.\n257\t\n258\tSuccess criteria: all 5 feedback workloads pass correctness, geomean speedup > 1.0 and improving,\n259\tconvergence when tiling/launch tweaks stop yielding gains → then `SEARCH_COMPLETE`. Final 16-workload\n260\teval is operator-only.\n261\t\n262\t---\n263\t\n264\t## 9. Candidate roadmap (sketch — full executable plan goes in docs/plan.md next)\n265\t\n266\t- **c001** — Fused two-launch tiled Triton GEMM (`Y = X·W^T`), `input_precision=\"tf32x3\"`, no\n267\t  cat/split, conservative tiling (e.g. `BM=128, BN=128, BK=32/64`, `warps=4`, `stages=3`),\n268\t  grouped-M scheduling. Establishes correctness + baseline speedup and validates the true-fp32\n269\t  reference assumption.\n270\t- **c002+** — Tiling/occupancy sweep around the winner (`BLOCK_*`, `num_warps`, `num_stages`,\n271\t  `GROUP_M`); possibly per-stream configs for the thin encoder (`M=77`) vs large image streams.\n272\t- **later** — launch-strategy alternative (single virtual-concat launch) if small-WL launch\n273\t  overhead matters; `ieee` accuracy variant kept as safety; consider `BLOCK_K`/pipeline tuning\n274\t  specific to the `tf32x3` 3× K-cost.\n275\t- Stop when improvement converges; write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`.\n276\t\n277\t---\n278\t\n279\t## 10. Skill applicability\n280\t\n281\t- `KernelWiki` — scoped to Blackwell (SM100/B200) and Hopper (SM90/H100). This target is **A800 /\n282\t  sm_80 (Ampere)**; the Blackwell/Hopper-specific techniques (tcgen05, TMEM, wgmma, CLC, 2-SM,\n283\t  NVFP4, FA4) do not apply. Not invoked for this draft.\n284\t- `ncu-report-skill` — profiling on **B200 / sm_100**, and direct profiler execution is disallowed\n285\t  here anyway. Not applicable.\n286\t- Skill usage for this task: **none / not applicable** (to be recorded as such in candidate rows).\n287\t\n288\t---\n289\t\n290\t## 11. Open questions / risks to watch\n291\t\n292\t1. **Reference precision** (true fp32 vs TF32) — governs both our speedup ceiling and accuracy\n293\t   safety. Strongly argued to be true fp32 by the tight atol; confirmed empirically by c001.\n294\t2. **`tf32x3` availability** in the installed Triton — if absent, drop to `ieee` (still removes\n295\t   cat; smaller but positive win) and note it.\n296\t3. **Small-M efficiency** — `M=77` encoder streams (WL3/WL5) may underutilize; watch whether they\n297\t   drag geomean and whether a thin-M config/launch helps.\n298\t4. **L2 pressure from `W` (37.7 MB ≈ L2)** — scheduling for `W` reuse across `M` tiles is\n299\t   important; grouped-M and large `BLOCK_M`.\n300\t5. **Contiguity assumptions** — if any feedback input is non-contiguous, handle via strides rather\n301\t   than a copy that reintroduces bandwidth cost.\n302\t6. **Two outputs, exact shapes/dtype** — must return `(processed_encoder [B,T,H], processed_hidden\n303\t   [B,I,H])` fp32, order and slicing semantics matching the reference exactly.\n304\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bcbb7765-926a-4d22-97a5-e083cb94ef12","uuid":"f83f784b-53a4-4af8-85a3-1d6760ed4195","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:59:11.269Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-030_flux_concatenated_sequence_processing_with_split/docs/draft.md","content":"# Draft — L2/030 Flux Concatenated Sequence Processing with Split\n\nRun: `formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-030_flux_concatenated_sequence_processing_with_split`\nTarget GPU: NVIDIA A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere). Framework: Triton primary; PyTorch only for\nmetadata/launch plumbing. No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension computational fallback.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Operation summary\n\nReference (`task/definition.json`) does, with `H = hidden_dim = 3072` (const):\n\n```\nconcatenated = torch.cat([encoder_hidden_states, hidden_states], dim=1)  # [B, T+I, H]\nprocessed    = concatenated @ process_weight.t()                          # [B, T+I, H]\nprocessed_encoder = processed[:, :T, :]                                   # [B, T, H]\nprocessed_hidden  = processed[:, T:, :]                                   # [B, I, H]\nreturn processed_encoder, processed_hidden\n```\n\nSymbols:\n- `B` = `batch_size` (var), `T` = `text_seq_len` (var), `I` = `img_seq_len` (var).\n- Inputs: `hidden_states` `[B, I, H]` fp32, `encoder_hidden_states` `[B, T, H]` fp32,\n  `process_weight` `W` `[H, H]` fp32 (an `nn.Linear`-style weight, `out=H`, `in=H`, no bias).\n- Outputs: `processed_encoder` `[B, T, H]` fp32, `processed_hidden` `[B, I, H]` fp32.\n\nThe task card calls this a **memory-bandwidth bottleneck** pattern from Flux dual-stream\ntransformer blocks: the `cat` materializes a fresh `[B, T+I, H]` tensor before the GEMM and the\n`split` slices it afterward.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Core algebraic simplification (the main lever)\n\nA linear projection is applied **independently per token row**. Concatenating along the sequence\n(row) dimension, projecting, then splitting back along the same row boundaries is a pure\nre-ordering that does **not** couple encoder rows with image rows. Therefore:\n\n```\nprocessed_encoder = encoder_hidden_states @ W.T      # [B, T, H]\nprocessed_hidden  = hidden_states         @ W.T      # [B, I, H]\n```\n\nis **mathematically identical** (row for row, elementwise) to the concat→matmul→split reference,\nwith the exact same output ordering. Consequences:\n\n- The `torch.cat` allocation + copy is **eliminated entirely** (this is the advertised bottleneck).\n- The `split` becomes trivial — we write each GEMM result straight into its own output tensor;\n  no post-hoc slicing/copy.\n- Both streams share the **same weight `W`**, so `W` stays hot in cache/registers across both.\n\nBoth projections are the same GEMM shape family:\n```\nX [M, K] · W^T [K, N]  ->  Y [M, N],   with  K = N = H = 3072\n```\nwhere `X` is a stream flattened to 2-D (`[B*T, H]` for encoder, `[B*I, H]` for image), and\n`W` is read as `W[n, k]` (i.e. `Y = X @ W.T`, per `nn.Linear`). Rows are fully independent, so\nbatch and sequence collapse into a single `M` dimension for contiguous inputs.\n\n**Equivalence conditions to preserve:**\n- Row order within each stream is preserved by flattening `[B, S, H] -> [B*S, H]` in C-contiguous\n  order and reshaping the output back the same way.\n- Encoder output uses encoder rows; image output uses image rows — never crossed.\n- Must produce **two** separate output tensors of the exact reference shapes/dtype.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Workload analysis (fixed feedback set)\n\n`H = 3072`, so every GEMM has `K = N = 3072`. `M_enc = B*T`, `M_img = B*I`,\n`M_total = B*(T+I)` (the reference's concatenated row count).\n\n| WL | uuid(prefix) | B | T | I | M_enc | M_img | M_total | atol | rtol | match |\n|----|--------------|---|-----|-----|-------|-------|---------|-------|-------|-------|\n| 1  | 7ae93ff7 | 2 | 1423 | 1489 | 2846 | 2978 | 5824 | 0.0021 | 1e-5 | 0.98 |\n| 2  | 7a91d659 | 4 | 128  | 256  | 512  | 1024 | 1536 | 0.002  | 1e-5 | 0.98 |\n| 3  | a9d58452 | 1 | 77   | 1024 | 77   | 1024 | 1101 | 0.0018 | 1e-5 | 0.98 |\n| 4  | 9de8abcb | 2 | 128  | 256  | 256  | 512  | 768  | 0.002  | 1e-5 | 0.98 |\n| 5  | e92f1fac | 1 | 77   | 4096 | 77   | 4096 | 4173 | 0.002  | 1e-5 | 0.98 |\n\nObservations:\n- `M_total` spans **768 → 5824** rows; `N = K = 3072` throughout. These are medium GEMMs, all\n  compute-heavy (large K,N) but with modest-to-small M.\n- Encoder streams can be **very small** (`M_enc = 77` in WL3/WL5) — a thin GEMM. The image stream\n  dominates FLOPs there. A single tiling config must remain efficient for both a 77-row and a\n  4096-row GEMM.\n- FLOPs per workload `= 2 * M_total * N * K`:\n  - WL1: `2·5824·3072·3072 ≈ 1.10e11` (110 GFLOP)\n  - WL2: `≈ 2.90e10`, WL3: `≈ 2.08e10`, WL4: `≈ 1.45e10`, WL5: `≈ 7.87e10`.\n- Tensor sizes (fp32, MB): `X_total = M_total·3072·4`; `W = 3072·3072·4 = 37.7 MB`.\n  - WL1: `X ≈ 71.5 MB` per side (in+out ≈ 143 MB) plus `W` 37.7 MB.\n  - `W` (37.7 MB) is close to A800 L2 (40 MB) — reuse of `W` across all `M` tiles is essential;\n    large `BLOCK_M` and good L2 residency of `W` matter.\n\n**Roofline sanity (A800):** peak fp32 SIMT ≈ 19.5 TFLOPS; TF32 tensor ≈ 156 TFLOPS; HBM ≈ 2 TB/s.\n- True-fp32 SIMT GEMM for WL1 ≈ `1.10e11 / 19.5e12 ≈ 5.6 ms`.\n- TF32-tensor GEMM for WL1 ≈ `0.7 ms` (but inaccurate — see §5).\n- 3×TF32 emulation (`tf32x3`) ≈ 1/3 of TF32 throughput ≈ 52 TFLOPS eff. → WL1 ≈ `2.1 ms`.\n- `cat` traffic for WL1 ≈ read(enc+img) + write(concat) ≈ `2 · 71.5 MB ≈ 143 MB` → ≈ `0.07 ms`\n  of pure bandwidth that our fused path deletes; relatively larger for the small workloads.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Constraints\n\n- **Dtype:** all fp32 in and out. Output tensors must be fp32 with reference shapes.\n- **Triton-only compute:** the GEMM must run in a Triton kernel. PyTorch may allocate outputs,\n  reshape/view, and launch. No `torch.matmul`/`F.linear`/cuBLAS as the computational path, no\n  CPU/NumPy, no CUDA C extension, no \"if Triton fails use torch\" fallback. A failed Triton\n  implementation is invalid, not fallback-able.\n- **Arch:** `sm_80`. No `tcgen05`/TMEM/wgmma/Hopper-Blackwell features. Ampere `mma` (incl.\n  TF32 tensor path) is the ceiling. `tl.dot` with fp32 inputs is the vehicle.\n- **Evaluation:** only `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`. Five workloads = one\n  candidate evaluation. Budget 100 evals; token soft 1.0M / hard 1.2M. Ranking = geomean speedup,\n  every selected workload must pass correctness. No direct CUDA/profiler/nvidia-smi runs.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Numerical risks (decisive for design)\n\nRandom `~N(0,1)` inputs; each output element is a length-3072 dot product, so\n`Var ≈ 3072`, i.e. output magnitude `≈ 55`. The pass rule (standard) is\n`|out - ref| ≤ atol + rtol·|ref|` for ≥ 98% of elements. Effective budget for `|ref| ≈ 55`:\n`≈ 0.002 + 1e-5·55 ≈ 0.00255`.\n\nPrecision options for `tl.dot` on fp32 inputs (Ampere):\n\n1. **Plain TF32 (`input_precision=\"tf32\"`)** — operands truncated to 10-bit mantissa\n   (`~5e-4` relative). Error of a 3072-term dot ≈ `sqrt(3072)·(~7e-4)·O(1) ≈ 0.04`, i.e.\n   **~0.04 absolute — ~16× over the 0.00255 budget on essentially every element.**\n   → **Rejected.** Fast (~0.7 ms WL1) but fails correctness badly.\n\n2. **3×TF32 (`input_precision=\"tf32x3\"`)** — decomposes each fp32 operand into 3 TF32 terms;\n   accuracy ≈ near-fp32 (relative `~1e-6`, i.e. absolute `~5e-5` at magnitude 55). **Comfortably\n   inside 0.00255.** Throughput ≈ 1/3 of TF32 tensor ≈ 52 TFLOPS eff → clearly beats fp32 SIMT.\n   → **Primary choice.** Best accuracy/speed trade-off.\n\n3. **IEEE fp32 (`input_precision=\"ieee\"`)** — true fp32 via CUDA-core FMA (no fp32 tensor core on\n   Ampere), `~19.5` TFLOPS. Exactly matches the reference numerically; same speed class as cuBLAS\n   fp32. Only wins from removing the `cat`. → **Fallback precision** if `tf32x3` is unavailable in\n   the installed Triton, or as an accuracy-safety candidate.\n\n**Why the reference must be true fp32 (not TF32):** with `atol ≈ 0.002` on magnitude-55 outputs,\nno tensor-core approximation could reproduce a TF32 reference to that tolerance. The tight atol\nis only self-consistent if the reference is computed in true fp32 and the atol is the *budget*\ngranted to approximate implementations. This is exactly the margin `tf32x3` needs and plain TF32\nblows. It also implies our `tf32x3` result (≈ true fp32) will land near the reference — safe.\n\n**Dependency / risk:** if, contrary to the above, the harness reference secretly ran with\n`allow_tf32=True` (TF32), then a near-fp32 `tf32x3` result could differ from it by `~0.04` and\nfail the 98% match. The tolerance analysis argues strongly against this, but the **first\nevaluation of c001 is the check**: if `tf32x3` fails correctness, that scenario (or a strides/\nshape bug) is the cause and we revisit. Accumulation is fp32 in all three modes (Triton accumulates\n`tl.dot` in fp32), so accumulation order is not a concern.\n\nOther numerical notes:\n- No bias, no activation, no reductions across tokens → no additional cancellation beyond the dot.\n- 2% mismatch slack (`0.98`) gives margin, but `tf32x3` should pass ~100% of elements.\n- Keep everything fp32; do not down-cast the accumulator or outputs.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Triton design space\n\n### 6.1 Kernel structure — tiled GEMM (`Y = X @ W.T`)\nStandard 2-D tiled matmul:\n- Program grid over `(M, N)` tiles: `BLOCK_M × BLOCK_N` output tile, reduce over `K` in\n  `BLOCK_K` chunks; accumulate in fp32; `tl.dot(x_tile, w_tile, acc, input_precision=...)`.\n- `W` read as `W[n, k]` so that `Y = X·W^T`; equivalently load `W` transposed tiles. Choose the\n  load pattern (via strides) that keeps `W` accesses coalesced; `W` is `[H, H]` contiguous.\n- `L2`-friendly tile scheduling (grouped-M / \"super-grouping\" of program ids) to maximize `W` and\n  `X` reuse — important because `W` (37.7 MB) barely fits L2.\n- Boundary masking on `M` (streams like `M=77` are not multiples of `BLOCK_M`); `N=K=3072` is a\n  multiple of common block sizes (128/64), so N/K masking may be avoidable for speed.\n\n### 6.2 Handling the two streams (launch strategy)\n- **Option A (baseline, simplest): two kernel launches**, one per stream, each over its flattened\n  `[M_side, H]`, writing directly into `processed_encoder` / `processed_hidden`. `W` is reused hot\n  across both launches. Launch overhead is negligible vs GEMM. **Preferred starting point.**\n- **Option B: one launch over a \"virtual\" concat.** A single grid of `M_total` row-tiles, where a\n  tile reads from encoder or image tensor depending on the global row index, and writes to the\n  matching output. Saves one launch but needs per-tile source selection (two base pointers +\n  branch or index math). Marginal benefit; consider only if launch overhead shows up for the\n  small workloads.\n- **Option C: batched/grouped GEMM.** Treat `(B, stream)` as groups. Overkill here since flatten\n  already collapses `B` into `M` for contiguous inputs; keep in reserve for non-contiguous cases.\n\nFor all options, avoid materializing the concatenation and avoid slicing copies.\n\n### 6.3 Precision knob\n- Start with `input_precision=\"tf32x3\"` (accuracy-safe, tensor-core speed).\n- Keep `input_precision=\"ieee\"` as an alternative candidate for an accuracy floor / if `tf32x3`\n  is missing in the installed Triton.\n- Never use plain `\"tf32\"` for the committed solution (fails tolerance) — could be used only as a\n  throwaway diagnostic to confirm the accuracy/speed trade-off, but not submitted.\n\n### 6.4 Tiling / occupancy search axes (for later candidates, not this draft)\n- `BLOCK_M ∈ {64, 128, 256}`, `BLOCK_N ∈ {64, 128, 256}`, `BLOCK_K ∈ {32, 64, 128}`.\n- `num_warps ∈ {4, 8}`, `num_stages ∈ {2, 3, 4, 5}` (software pipelining of K-loop; watch shared\n  memory pressure with fp32 tiles on `sm_80`, which has 164 KB/SM configurable smem).\n- Grouped-M scheduling `GROUP_M ∈ {4, 8}` for L2 reuse.\n- Consider a **thin-M specialization** for tiny encoder streams (`M=77`): small `BLOCK_M` (e.g. 64)\n  to avoid wasting rows, or fold the small stream differently. Possibly different configs per\n  stream (encoder vs image) since their `M` differ by 1–2 orders of magnitude.\n- `tf32x3` roughly triples the effective K-work; `BLOCK_K` and pipelining interact with that.\n\n### 6.5 What NOT to do\n- No split-K unless a workload proves K-parallelism starved (K=3072 with these M/N is fine and\n  split-K needs an fp32 atomic/second-pass reduction that risks accuracy/complexity).\n- No fp16/bf16 down-cast (blows tolerance).\n- No cuBLAS/`torch.matmul` compute path.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Baseline cost model & expected speedup\n\nReference cost ≈ `cat` (bandwidth) + `matmul` (true-fp32 SIMT, ~19.5 TFLOPS) + `split` (views,\n≈ free). Our fused path ≈ `matmul` (`tf32x3`, ~52 TFLOPS eff) with **no** `cat`, writing outputs\ndirectly.\n\n- Matmul speedup ceiling ≈ `52 / 19.5 ≈ 2.7×` (if reference is true-fp32 SIMT and cuBLAS is not\n  itself tensor-accelerated). Plus the deleted `cat` traffic (a few percent on large WL, more on\n  the small WL where the GEMM is short and copies are relatively bigger).\n- Realistic geomean target: meaningful (> ~1.5×) if the reference is true fp32; the exact number\n  depends on how close cuBLAS fp32 SIMT is to peak and on our tiling efficiency at small M.\n- **Falsifiable checkpoint:** if c001 (`tf32x3`, fused) is *slower* than the reference, the most\n  likely explanation is that the reference matmul is already tensor-accelerated (TF32) — in which\n  case our accuracy would also be at risk and we would re-examine assumptions rather than chase\n  tiling.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Validation strategy\n\nConstraints: no direct CUDA/profiler/nvidia-smi/alternate harness; the trusted evaluator is the\nonly correctness+timing oracle, invoked as `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` over\nall five fixed workloads (= one evaluation).\n\nLayered validation:\n1. **Algebraic equivalence (static, this draft):** concat→matmul→split ≡ per-stream matmul; row\n   order preserved by C-contiguous flatten/reshape; two outputs of exact reference shape/dtype.\n   Documented above (§2).\n2. **Shape/stride audit before each eval:** confirm handling of `[B, S, H]` contiguous inputs,\n   flatten to `[B*S, H]`, reshape outputs back to `[B, S, H]`; handle `W` as `[H, H]` with correct\n   transpose semantics; guard `T=0`/`I=0` (not in feedback set but cheap to guard). Prefer passing\n   strides to the kernel over forcing `.contiguous()` copies; only copy if a stream is\n   non-contiguous (feedback inputs are expected contiguous).\n3. **Numerical margin reasoning (§5):** `tf32x3` error `~5e-5` ≪ budget `~0.00255`; expect ~100%\n   element match. c001 doubles as the empirical confirmation that the reference is true fp32.\n4. **Evaluator-driven loop:** each immutable candidate `cNNN` evaluated once over the 5 workloads;\n   record per-workload correctness (must pass) and speedup, geomean, decision, cumulative eval\n   count, parent, source hash, hypothesis, skill usage in `candidates.jsonl` (append-only).\n5. **Regression discipline:** change one axis per candidate (precision → then tiling → then launch\n   strategy) so each eval attributes cause cleanly; never reuse an id for changed source.\n6. **Accuracy safety net:** keep an `ieee`-precision variant ready; if any workload fails\n   correctness under `tf32x3`, evaluate the `ieee` variant to isolate accuracy vs. bug.\n\nSuccess criteria: all 5 feedback workloads pass correctness, geomean speedup > 1.0 and improving,\nconvergence when tiling/launch tweaks stop yielding gains → then `SEARCH_COMPLETE`. Final 16-workload\neval is operator-only.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Candidate roadmap (sketch — full executable plan goes in docs/plan.md next)\n\n- **c001** — Fused two-launch tiled Triton GEMM (`Y = X·W^T`), `input_precision=\"tf32x3\"`, no\n  cat/split, conservative tiling (e.g. `BM=128, BN=128, BK=32/64`, `warps=4`, `stages=3`),\n  grouped-M scheduling. Establishes correctness + baseline speedup and validates the true-fp32\n  reference assumption.\n- **c002+** — Tiling/occupancy sweep around the winner (`BLOCK_*`, `num_warps`, `num_stages`,\n  `GROUP_M`); possibly per-stream configs for the thin encoder (`M=77`) vs large image streams.\n- **later** — launch-strategy alternative (single virtual-concat launch) if small-WL launch\n  overhead matters; `ieee` accuracy variant kept as safety; consider `BLOCK_K`/pipeline tuning\n  specific to the `tf32x3` 3× K-cost.\n- Stop when improvement converges; write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Skill applicability\n\n- `KernelWiki` — scoped to Blackwell (SM100/B200) and Hopper (SM90/H100). This target is **A800 /\n  sm_80 (Ampere)**; the Blackwell/Hopper-specific techniques (tcgen05, TMEM, wgmma, CLC, 2-SM,\n  NVFP4, FA4) do not apply. Not invoked for this draft.\n- `ncu-report-skill` — profiling on **B200 / sm_100**, and direct profiler execution is disallowed\n  here anyway. Not applicable.\n- Skill usage for this task: **none / not applicable** (to be recorded as such in candidate rows).\n\n---\n\n## 11. Open questions / risks to watch\n\n1. **Reference precision** (true fp32 vs TF32) — governs both our speedup ceiling and accuracy\n   safety. Strongly argued to be true fp32 by the tight atol; confirmed empirically by c001.\n2. **`tf32x3` availability** in the installed Triton — if absent, drop to `ieee` (still removes\n   cat; smaller but positive win) and note it.\n3. **Small-M efficiency** — `M=77` encoder streams (WL3/WL5) may underutilize; watch whether they\n   drag geomean and whether a thin-M config/launch helps.\n4. **L2 pressure from `W` (37.7 MB ≈ L2)** — scheduling for `W` reuse across `M` tiles is\n   important; grouped-M and large `BLOCK_M`.\n5. **Contiguity assumptions** — if any feedback input is non-contiguous, handle via strides rather\n   than a copy that reintroduces bandwidth cost.\n6. **Two outputs, exact shapes/dtype** — must return `(processed_encoder [B,T,H], processed_hidden\n   [B,I,H])` fp32, order and slicing semantics matching the reference exactly.\n","numLines":304,"startLine":1,"totalLines":304}}}
